diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk ticket claims PC11 (172.16.20.11, VLAN 20) is completely isolated, with its switch port shut off. Live evidence does not support this. Phase 1 reachability probes show pc11 reaching pc10 (172.16.20.10, same-VLAN neighbour) and pc5 (172.16.10.5, a different VLAN entirely) with 0% packet loss. Every diagnostic layer was examined:

**L2/Infrastructure (Phase A):** infra_sweep scanned all 28 devices and flagged zero anomalies — pc11 has a valid IP (172.16.20.11), a default route, no ACL drops, and no ARP poisoning. switch2.eth5 (the pc11 access port, `access_vlan20_port6`) carries no link-down history. l2_snapshot found zero duplicate MACs. The VLAN20 segment is entirely healthy.

**Control plane (Phase B):** All nine OSPF/FRR routers are Full-adjacency with 18 routes each. No shaped or corrupted qdiscs appear on any path interface.

**Resource/service (Phase D):** pressure_sweep found no stress tools, CPU spikes, or daemon failures on any device.

**Root cause:** No fault is present. The network is fully operational. PC11's switch port on switch2 is UP, VLAN 20 is correctly configured end-to-end, and OSPF routing is propagating all prefixes normally. The user's report appears to describe either a past transient that has since self-recovered or a misidentification of the affected device. No corrective action is needed on the network side; the operator should verify at the physical or OS level of the end-user machine (e.g., NIC driver, OS networking, cable seating) if the user still experiences a problem at their desk.
=== END ===